Package: grin
Title: Amortised Inference for General Recognition Theory
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R: c(
    person("Murray S.", "Bennett", email = "murray.bennett92@gmail.com", role = c("aut", "cre")),
    person("Peter D.", "Kvam", role = "aut"),
    person("Joseph W.", "Houpt", role = "aut"))
Description: Amortised, uncertainty-calibrated inference of General Recognition
    Theory (GRT) perceptual representations from a 2x2 identification confusion
    matrix. A neural network, trained once on simulated data and shipped as a
    TorchScript graph, replaces per-participant maximum-likelihood fitting:
    milliseconds instead of seconds, with a calibrated posterior over the 12
    identified GRT parameters plus construct probabilities (perceptual
    independence, perceptual separability), a stopping-rule API for adaptive
    designs, and a plotting suite (individual perceptual space, parameter, and
    construct-probability plots; group-level space, distribution, model-class,
    and precision plots) for reporting results at every level from one
    participant to a full sample. Inference runs natively via the 'torch'
    package (libtorch) -- no Python is required. Companion to the 'grintools'
    Python package; both wrap the same trained weights, numerically verified
    to agree (see <https://github.com/MurraySBennett/grin>).
